 "web_search": true,
 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T07:01+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, depending on who you are and how long you plan to be outside, wearing a mask (specifically an N95, KN95, FFP2, or KF94) is strongly advised.** \n\nSingapore is currently experiencing a severe haze episode driven by smoke from regional fires, with the 24-hour Pollutant Standards Index (PSI) hitting \"Unhealthy\" to \"Very Unhealthy\" levels across multiple regions (and peaking at over 220 in parts of the island). \n\nThe National Environment Agency (NEA) and Ministry of Health (MOH) guidelines regarding outdoor exposure and masks include the following recommendations:\n\n* **For Vulnerable Groups** (Elderly, pregnant women, children, and individuals with chronic heart or lung conditions like asthma): \n  * You should **minimize or avoid outdoor activities** altogether while PSI levels are this high. \n  * If you *must* be outdoors for several hours when air quality is in the very unhealthy range, **you should wear an N95 (or equivalent) mask**. Standard surgical or cloth masks are not effective against fine haze particles (PM2.5). *(Note: If you have severe heart or respiratory issues that make breathing difficult, consult your doctor before using an N95 mask.)*\n* **For Healthy Persons:** \n  * You should avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor physical exertion. \n  * While casual, short-duration trips outside don't strictly mandate an N95 for healthy individuals, wearing one can help reduce your exposure if you are sensitive to smog or need to spend extended periods outdoors.\n\nBefore heading out, check real-time air quality metrics and localized 1-hour PM2.5 readings via the official **myENV app** or **[www.haze.gov.sg](https://www.haze.gov.sg)**, as air quality can fluctuate throughout the day.",
